+  "body": "## A Letter from Alexandria\n\nIn 1 BC a man named Hilarion, who was working in Alexandria and waiting on his wages, wrote a letter home to a woman named Alis. He calls her his sister, but most readers take her to be his wife. She was expecting a child.\n\nThe letter is short and mostly kind. He tells her not to worry if the others go home and he stays on in Alexandria. He asks her to take care of the small child they already have, and he promises to send money as soon as he is paid. In the middle of all this is the sentence that made the letter famous. In one English translation it reads: \"if you bear a child and it is male, let it be; if it is female, cast it out.\" A few lines later he answers a message she had sent him through someone named Aphrodisias, asking him not to forget her. How could he forget her? he asks. He begs her once more not to worry.\n\nThe letter was found at Oxyrhynchus, a town in Middle Egypt, among the papyri dug out of its ancient rubbish mounds, and scholars know it as P.Oxy. 744. Hilarion isn't a monster, and that is what makes the letter hard to read. He is a working man far from home, worried about money, tender toward his wife, fond of the child he already has. The instruction about the baby sits in the letter as one more household arrangement, between the wages and the reassurance.\n\nTo cast out a child, or expose it, as historians usually say, was to leave a newborn outside, often somewhere public where it might be found, and walk away. Some of those babies died of cold or hunger or animals. Some were picked up by strangers, and many of those were raised as slaves. Hilarion did not have to defend the practice to his wife. Few people around him would have expected him to.\n\n## Why Roman Families Left Children Outside\n\nChristians who tell this story usually hurry to the contrast. But most Romans were not cruel people, and Alis's position was a hard one. She had no reliable way to prevent a pregnancy. Childbirth itself was dangerous, and a great many babies died in their first year whatever anyone did. A laborer's household lived close to the edge, and one more mouth could mean less food for the child already at the table. A son might work and support his parents when they were old. A daughter would need a dowry if she was to marry, and she would leave for another household. There were no orphanages and no adoption agencies. A family that raised every child it conceived could go under, and take the children it already had down with it.\n\nMany Romans also didn't think of exposure as killing. The historian John Boswell argued that the English word carries a sense of harm the ancient words lacked, and he preferred to call the practice abandonment. Augustine, writing in North Africa four centuries after Hilarion, described parents who left their infants \"in order to their being cared for by any passer-by.\" A parent could tell himself that he had handed the child to fortune or the gods, and that some childless couple, or some household that needed hands, would come by. Sometimes one did. It's easy to call that self-deception, and much of it was. But anyone who has made a terrible choice under pressure knows how such a story gets told, and how badly a person needs to believe it.\n\nFew of us in Hilarion's place, with no wages yet and a wife at home with a small child, could be sure we would not have written the same sentence. That's why the Christian difference is worth studying. The Christians who refused to put their children out carried the same pressures Hilarion did, but they lived by a different account of what a child is.\n\n## What the First Christians Taught\n\nThe earliest Christian teaching on the subject is blunt. The Didache, a short manual of Christian teaching and one of the oldest Christian writings outside the New Testament, lists what a believer must not do. The list begins with murder and adultery, and partway through it says, in Kirsopp Lake's translation, \"thou shalt not procure abortion, nor commit infanticide\" (Didache 2.2). The child in the womb and the child just born appear in the same line, in the same list as murder.\n\nAn anonymous letter to a man named Diognetus, probably written in the second century, explains to an outsider how Christians live. They live in Greek and foreign cities alike, the writer says, and they follow the local customs in clothing and food and the rest of ordinary life. Then he names what sets them apart, and the first custom he names concerns their children: \"They marry as all men, they bear children, but they do not expose their offspring\" (Diognetus 5.6, again in Lake's translation). He treats it as one of the few things that would let a neighbor tell a Christian household from any other.\n\nJustin Martyr made the same point to the emperor. His First Apology, addressed to Antoninus Pius in the 150s, tells him that \"we have been taught that to expose newly-born children is the part of wicked men.\" His first reasons aren't the ones a modern reader expects. Christians refuse, he says, because \"almost all so exposed (not only the girls, but also the males) are brought up to prostitution,\" and a man who visits prostitutes may be lying with his own abandoned child without knowing it. A little later he gives the plainest reason of all. Christians will not expose a child \"lest some of them be not picked up, but die, and we become murderers\" (First Apology 27 and 29). Justin knew where the foundlings of his city ended up, and he wrote it down for the emperor to read.\n\nTertullian went further. In his Apology, written at Carthage around 197, he took up the charge that Christians killed and ate babies at their secret meetings, and he turned it back on the people who made it. How many of the crowd shouting for Christian blood, he asked, and how many even of the rulers who had been severe with Christians, could he charge in their own consciences \"with the sin of putting their offspring to death\"? He named the methods: \"by drowning, or by exposure to cold and hunger and dogs.\" For Christians, he wrote, \"murder being once for all forbidden, we may not destroy even the foetus in the womb\" (Apology 9). The people accused of eating children lived by a rule that would not let them put a child out at all.\n\nThe Christians did not invent this conviction. They inherited it. Jews had long refused to kill their newborns, and even Tacitus, who wrote about the Jews with open contempt early in the second century, recorded that \"they count it a crime to kill any of their later-born children\" (Histories 5.5). What the church added was a story that made the old conviction hard to set aside.\n\n## Why Christians Saw Children Differently\n\nPart of that story was creation. Genesis says that God created man in his own image, \"male and female he created them\" (Genesis 1:27). Hilarion's sentence sorted children along exactly that line, and the Christians would not. Every child, girl or boy, sickly or strong, bore God's image, and no household could decide that one of them was surplus.\n\nThen there was the way Jesus treated children. When people brought them to him and the disciples rebuked them, Mark says Jesus \"was indignant\" and told them, \"Let the children come to me; do not hinder them, for to such belongs the kingdom of God.\" Then \"he took them in his arms and blessed them, laying his hands on them\" (Mark 10:14, 16). In Matthew he sets a child in the middle of his disciples and says, \"Whoever receives one such child in my name receives me\" (Matthew 18:5).\n\nThe deepest part of it was older. Israel had long told its own story as the story of an unwanted child. In Ezekiel 16 the Lord describes Jerusalem as a baby left to die on the day of its birth: \"No eye pitied you, to do any of these things to you out of compassion for you, but you were cast out on the open field, for you were abhorred, on the day that you were born.\" Then God comes by: \"And when I passed by you and saw you wallowing in your blood, I said to you in your blood, 'Live!'\" (Ezekiel 16:5–6). The rest of the chapter is a hard charge against the city's unfaithfulness, but it begins there. The people of God started out as a foundling that God picked up.\n\nMoses is the same story told another way. Pharaoh ordered, \"Every son that is born to the Hebrews you shall cast into the Nile\" (Exodus 1:22). The boy who would lead Israel out of Egypt survived because his mother set him in a basket among the reeds and Pharaoh's own daughter found him. \"She took pity on him,\" Exodus says (Exodus 2:6). The early Christians read those stories, and they read Paul telling them that God sent his Son \"so that we might receive adoption as sons\" (Galatians 4:5). They understood themselves as children who had been taken in. People who believe that about themselves find it very hard to put a baby out.\n\n## Did the Early Christians Rescue Abandoned Babies?\n\nHere the story usually told in churches runs ahead of the evidence. You may have heard that the early Christians went out to the rubbish heaps at night and gathered up the babies left there. It's a moving picture, but it isn't what the early writers quoted here describe. What they describe is refusal. Christians would not expose their own children, and they said so publicly, to emperors and to the crowds that accused them.\n\nEvidence that they took in other people's foundlings comes later. In a letter written in 408, Augustine mentions in passing that foundlings were sometimes picked up by women vowed to virginity, who had no children of their own, and brought to be baptized. In 442 a council of bishops at Vaison, in Gaul, made rules about abandoned infants. In the West, homes for foundlings came later still; a document dated 787 credits an archpriest of Milan named Datheus with founding one, though historians have questioned the date.\n\nAugustine's letter also shows that nearly a century after Constantine's conversion, in a Christian province, parents were still leaving children for passers-by. The Christian emperors changed the law slowly. A law of Constantine in 331 let people who took in a child its father had cast out keep it, as a son or as a slave, as they chose. In 374 a law issued under Valentinian I made the killing of an infant a capital crime, and historians think exposure itself was probably outlawed the same year. It went on anyway.\n\nHistorians disagree about almost every number in this story. Boswell estimated in *The Kindness of Strangers* that urban Romans of the first three centuries abandoned somewhere between 20 and 40 percent of their children, but argued that most of those children were taken in by others and did not die. He also argued that abandonment stayed common in Christian Europe until the Renaissance, and that it changed form more than it disappeared: parents gave children to monasteries, and later left them at foundling hospitals.\n\nW. V. Harris agreed that exposure was widespread, but his study of child exposure in the Roman Empire read the evidence darker. He described it as \"very often but by no means always resulting in death,\" and as a way of moving children who were born free into slavery. Earlier, Donald Engels had argued that a high rate of female infanticide was demographically impossible, and Harris had replied that the argument didn't hold. The sociologist Rodney Stark built part of his account of the church's growth on the difference: pagan families exposed more girls than boys, Christians refused, and so the churches came to have more women of childbearing age and grew faster. His argument rests on the very estimates the historians are still contesting.\n\nWhat can be said with confidence is narrower than the sermon illustration and still remarkable. From its earliest documents, the church taught that a newborn could not be put out. It said so to emperors who could have its members killed, and it treated the refusal as one of the marks of a Christian household. Rescue came later and unevenly. Abandonment did not end when the empire became Christian, and Christians practiced it too.\n\n## What the Church Did with Foundlings and Their Mothers\n\nIf the early chapters of this history are less heroic than we like to tell them, some of the later chapters are much worse. Over the centuries the church's care for abandoned children grew into institutions, and some of those institutions became places where children died in great numbers and their mothers were punished for having them.\n\nIn Italy, as the historian David Kertzer showed in *Sacrificed for Honor*, church rules meant to stop abortion and infanticide and to spare unmarried mothers disgrace became, over time, laws that made single women give up their babies as soon as they were born. Hundreds of thousands of those children died in foundling homes.\n\nIn Ireland, a state commission reported in January 2021 on eighteen institutions, fourteen mother and baby homes and four county homes. About 56,000 unmarried mothers and 57,000 children had passed through them, and about 9,000 of the children died, roughly 15 percent. At the home in Tuam run by the Bon Secours Sisters, the local historian Catherine Corless found death records for 796 children who died there in the years it was open, from 1925 to 1961.\n\nIt would be easy for a Protestant to read that and feel safely distant. Bethany Home in Dublin was run by evangelical Protestants, most of them from the Church of Ireland, the Presbyterians and the Plymouth Brethren. Research published in 2010 found that more than two hundred of its children, who died between 1922 and 1949, lay in unmarked graves in Dublin's Mount Jerome Cemetery.\n\nIn Canada, most of the maternity homes for unmarried women in the decades after the Second World War were tied to the Catholic Church and the Salvation Army, and others to the Anglican and United churches. In the United States, Ann Fessler's oral history *The Girls Who Went Away* gathered the stories of women who surrendered babies between 1945 and 1973, years in which, by the estimate cited with her book, about a million and a half babies were given up for adoption. The women she interviewed were not throwing their children away. Many of them were pressured into giving them up.\n\nI don't know how to read these histories without seeing Hilarion's letter in them. Hilarion decided for Alis what would happen if the baby was a girl. The homes decided for the mothers. The church that had once refused to put children out learned, in too many places, to put out the women who bore them outside marriage, and in some of those places the children did not survive either.\n\nJesus said that whoever receives a child in his name receives him. In the next breath he warned that it would be better to be drowned with a great millstone around your neck than to cause \"one of these little ones who believe in me to sin\" (Matthew 18:6). A girl taught by her church that her baby was a disgrace, and that she was one too, was being taught a lie about God. Jesus' warning covers that kind of harm.\n\n## The Unwanted Child in Post-Christian America\n\nNobody in this country leaves newborns on a rubbish heap as an accepted custom. Every state now has a safe haven law that lets a parent leave a newborn safely at a hospital or another designated place, such as a fire station. Texas passed the first in 1999, and it's still called the Baby Moses law. But children who cannot stay with their own families are still with us. On the last day of September 2024, more than 325,000 children were in foster care in the United States, by the federal government's count. More than fifteen thousand young people aged out of foster care in 2025 without a permanent family, by the National Council for Adoption's count. Most of those children came into the state's care because a family could not, for a time or for good, keep them safe.\n\nAnd there is abortion. The Didache and Tertullian drew no line between the child in the womb and the child on the doorstep, and the church's historic opposition to abortion goes back to them, among people who had no power to make any law at all. Americans are deeply divided over what the law should say, and Christians are divided too. This essay won't settle that argument. It is after the care for mothers and children that both sides of it are tempted to leave undone.\n\nA church's politics can blind it to the parts of Scripture its side would rather not hear, and that is as true [on the progressive side](/writing/the-progressive-blind-spot) as [on the conservative one](/writing/the-conservative-blind-spot). On this subject the blind spot is not hard to find. Some Christians will speak up for the unborn child and never ask what a pregnant seventeen-year-old in their own youth group would need in order to keep her baby. Others will speak up for that same girl and look away from the child she is carrying. Plenty of Christians do better than either picture. They staff pregnancy centers, become foster parents, adopt, sit with frightened mothers in hospital rooms, and push for prenatal care and paid leave. But the pull is real, and it tends to run along the same line our voting does.\n\nThe church before Constantine had no political power to use for anyone. I've written elsewhere about [how it lived under Rome without it](/writing/the-early-church-under-rome). What it had was households, and a teaching it was willing to be seen keeping. What would that look like in an American congregation now? It would start with a church where an unmarried, pregnant teenager could tell her youth leader without dreading it, and be met with help before she heard a lecture. More families would become foster parents, and many more would carry meals, babysit and give a night off to the foster parents already worn thin. Adoption would honor the birth mother instead of erasing her, and churches would keep helping a