the Bible \"to prefer those that are received by all the catholic churches to those which some do not receive.\" And it had to agree with the rule of faith, the summary of apostolic teaching that new Christians confessed at their baptism.\n\nThe tests worked slowly, and they could correct a mistake. Around 200, Serapion, bishop of Antioch, found the church at Rhossus quarreling over a gospel bearing Peter's name. He hadn't read it, and he told them that if that was all they were divided over, they could read it. Then he learned it had been used to teach a false view of Christ. He got a copy, read it through, and found much in it that was sound and some things added. He wrote to them: \"For we, brethren, receive both Peter and the other apostles as Christ; but we reject intelligently the writings falsely ascribed to them, knowing that such were not handed down to us.\"\n\nWhen councils finally spoke, they spoke for their own region and in the language of receiving. The North African bishops met at Hippo in 393, with Augustine present, and at Carthage in 397. They named the books to be read in church as Scripture. When they gathered their rulings into one code in 419, they asked that the list be sent to Boniface, the bishop of Rome, and to other bishops \"that they may confirm this canon, for these are the things which we have received from our fathers to be read in church.\"\n\n## Bart Ehrman's Case\n\nThe scholarly version of the skeptic's case doesn't need Constantine at all. Bart Ehrman, a New Testament scholar who taught for many years at the University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill, made it in his 2003 book *Lost Christianities*. The second century, he argues, held many Christianities. Some believers kept the Jewish law and saw Jesus as a human messiah chosen by God. Marcion's churches had two gods. Groups often called Gnostic taught secret knowledge and read gospels of their own. One group won. Ehrman calls it proto-orthodox. It set the criteria and judged the books, and its historians, Eusebius among them, told the story afterward. On this reading, the rule of faith is the winners' theology under a reverent name, so of course the books that passed agreed with it. Ehrman builds on Walter Bauer, who argued in 1934 that in some regions what was later called heresy had come first.\n\nMuch of that is true. The second century was more varied than many Christians imagine. Eusebius took a side. Some of the losing books survive only in their opponents' summaries, and some came back by accident, like the full text of the Gospel of Thomas, found in a buried collection near Nag Hammadi in Egypt in 1945. Even the apostolic test was hard to apply. Eusebius recorded the doubts about 2 Peter: \"we have learned that his extant second Epistle does not belong to the canon; yet, as it has appeared profitable to many, it has been used with the other Scriptures.\" Scholars still argue over who wrote it. [Can You Trust the Bible?](/writing/why-trust-the-bible) looks at Ehrman's work on how the manuscripts were copied.\n\nBut the evidence also limits his case. By the end of the second century the four Gospels and Paul's letters were being read in churches from Gaul to Egypt. That was more than a hundred years before Constantine took any interest, and no central office imposed them. Most scholars date the four Gospels to the first century and the Gospel of Thomas to the second. And the rule of faith is older than the second century's quarrels. The earliest Christian writings we have are Paul's letters from the 50s. In one of them he passes on what he says he himself received, \"that Christ died for our sins in accordance with the Scriptures, that he was buried, that he was raised on the third day in accordance with the Scriptures\" (1 Corinthians 15:3–4). That confession binds Jesus to Israel's Scriptures and to a real death and resurrection, the very things Marcion and many Gnostic teachers denied. The standard the churches used to judge books came from the oldest texts they had.\n\nA party shaping a collection to win an argument would hardly have kept four Gospels that are hard to fit together, or a letter whose author no one knew. It would not have left its doubts in writing for anyone to find. The early church was diverse, and its judgments were human ones. They were made in the open, by churches that disagreed with each other and said so, about books already read in worship for generations.\n\n## So Who Decided?\n\nNo single person or meeting decided. The churches recognized books that had already proved themselves in worship. The lists came after the reading. Protestant and Catholic teaching weigh that recognition differently, but both describe the church as receiving these books, not making them Scripture. The Church of England's articles put it simply: \"All the Books of the New Testament, as they are commonly received, we do receive, and account them Canonical.\" The First Vatican Council taught in 1870 that the church holds these books sacred because they were written under the inspiration of the Holy Spirit, not because the church approved them afterward.\n\nPaul thanked God that the Thessalonians, when they heard the apostles' preaching, \"accepted it not as the word of men but as what it really is, the word of God, which is at work in you believers\" (1 Thessalonians 2:13). The lists drawn up centuries later record the same kind of hearing on a larger scale. The churches had found these books at work among them, Sunday after Sunday.\n\nIf you're a Christian who felt shaken when someone told you a council voted on your Bible, you can set that fear down. The process was slow and human, and Christians disagreed along the way. If you're a skeptic, the history won't make you believe. It does mean the question can't be settled by blaming an emperor. Read Athanasius's letter, and then read the books for yourself in the [Study Bible](/study/bible).\n\nThe Christians Justin described read the apostles and prophets \"as long as time permits.\" Many American churches now read a few verses on a Sunday and move on. We will argue for every book in the canon and still hear very little of it read aloud.\n\nThe churches held on to these books because they kept hearing a voice in them.\n\nIt was the voice of the risen Christ, who on the evening of the resurrection opened the Law and the Prophets and the Psalms to his frightened disciples.\n\nHe still speaks through all of it, the Old Testament as much as the New, to anyone who will read it.\n\n*\"For whatever was written in former days was written for our instruction, that through endurance and through the encouragement of the Scriptures we might have hope.\"* (Romans 15:4)\n\n*\"My sheep hear my voice, and I know them, and they follow me.\"* (John 10:27)\n",
   "updated": "2026-10-28T00:00:00.000Z"
 }
