 He sailed for home that July, weeks before the war began. He was arrested in 1943, and on April 9, 1945, a month before the war in Europe ended, he was hanged at Flossenbürg. He and Douglass stood in very different places, one shut out of his nation's promises by its laws, the other bound to a nation ruled by a criminal regime. Each told his country the truth at real cost, and each did it from inside, as a man who still said "this country" or "our national history" and meant it.
 
-## Two Ways to Get It Wrong
+## Worshiping a Country and Despising It
 
 There are two ways to fail at this love. The first is worship, which treats the country as beyond judgment and hears criticism as treason. It reads God's favor off the nation's successes and blames its failures on enemies within. In church it can make the flag something close to a sacrament, until loyalty to the nation and faithfulness to Christ are hard to tell apart. In our day this disorder lives mostly on the right, though not only there, and it is the one Barth found in his teachers' signatures. It is the man in Chesterton's line who says his mother is his mother "drunk or sober" and means he doesn't care which.
 
-Contempt is the other failure. It sees the country as nothing but its crimes and can name every sin in the nation's history without giving thanks for a single gift. It looks down on neighbors who still love the place, quick to say that they have sinned and slow to say, with Nehemiah, "I and my father's house have sinned." Today it shows up mostly on the left and among the young, but not only there. Contempt feels like moral seriousness. More often than its owners suspect, it is pride, and underneath the pride is ingratitude.
+Contempt is the other failure. It sees the country as nothing but its crimes and can name every sin in the nation's history without giving thanks for a single gift. It looks down on neighbors who still love the place, quick to say that they have sinned and slow to say, with Nehemiah, "I and my father's house have sinned" (Nehemiah 1:6). Today it shows up mostly on the left, but not only there. Contempt feels like moral seriousness. More often than its owners suspect, it is pride, and underneath the pride is ingratitude.
 
 They look like opposites, but they grow from one root. Each, in its own way, asks the nation to be the kingdom of God. The worshiper believes it nearly is. The despiser never quite forgives the country for being only a country, a mixed and mortal thing. Gallup's numbers hint at the shared root. Between 2024 and 2025 the share of Republicans who said they were extremely or very proud to be American rose from 85 to 92 percent, while among Democrats it fell from 62 to 36. Whatever else changed that year, the party in the White House did. When love of country rises and falls with an election, it was never quite love of the country.
 
-The two disorders have not done equal damage, and it would be false to pretend they have. Worship of the nation has blessed armies, and in Douglass's America it lent slavery the language of worship. Contempt has far fewer dead to its name. But it does its own harm. It cuts the church off from the neighbors it is sent to love, who can tell when they are despised, and it hands the whole subject of patriotism to the people most likely to abuse it. Most of us carry some of each, and which one shows depends a good deal on who is in power. I know that's true of me.
+The two disorders have not done equal damage. Worship of the nation has blessed wars without asking whether they were just, and in Douglass's America it offered prayers and hymns for a republic that held slaves. Contempt has far fewer dead to its name. But it does its own harm. It cuts the church off from the neighbors it is sent to love, who can tell when they are despised, and it hands the whole subject of patriotism to the people most likely to abuse it. Most of us carry some of each, and which one shows depends a good deal on who is in power. I know that's true of me.
 
 ## Loving a Country Like a Family
 
 The best picture of a Christian love of country is the one Chesterton reached for, which is the love of a family. You didn't choose your family, and you owe it more than you can repay. When it's in trouble, you stay. You know its faults better than any outsider does, and you say them out loud because you want it to be better. But you don't worship it. Jesus set that last limit as plainly as anything He said: "Whoever loves father or mother more than me is not worthy of me" (Matthew 10:37). If love of father and mother has a ceiling, love of country has one too.
 
-In *Orthodoxy*, published in 1908, Chesterton asked what would help a London district he called "a desperate thing," Pimlico, and answered that only love would: "If men loved Pimlico as mothers love children, arbitrarily, because it is THEIRS, Pimlico in a year or two might be fairer than Florence." Then he put it as history: "Men did not love Rome because she was great. She was great because they had loved her." Love comes first, and the improvement follows. That is how Nehemiah loved a city in ruins.
+In *Orthodoxy* Chesterton asked what would help a London district he called "a desperate thing," Pimlico, and answered that only love would: "If men loved Pimlico as mothers love children, arbitrarily, because it is THEIRS, Pimlico in a year or two might be fairer than Florence." Then he put it as history: "Men did not love Rome because she was great. She was great because they had loved her." Love comes first, and the improvement follows. That is how Nehemiah loved a city in ruins.
 
 In practice this love is quiet. You pray for your country by name and thank God for particular gifts rather than for an idea of America. You learn its history well enough to know the courage and the cruelty in it, including [what the founders actually built](/writing/was-america-founded-as-a-christian-nation), and you teach your children both. The neighbor down the street is the country in its most concrete form, and you serve him whatever his politics. When the country disappoints you, you stay in the argument instead of leaving it to the people who will only flatter it or only curse it. And on Sunday you keep the church's worship fixed on Christ.
 
